---
import Command from './Command.astro';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import { doc, links, version, zipName } from '../../data/site';
---

<section class="section" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
  <div class="wrap start">
    <div class="start__text">
      <p class="kicker"><b>07</b> Get started</p>
      <h2 id="start-title">From download to first run</h2>
      <p>One zip, one installer, one command. Model weights are not included, so have them ready.</p>

      <ul class="needs">
        <li><b>Windows 11.</b> The window uses WebView2, which ships with it. Only Windows 11 is verified.</li>
        <li><b>An NVIDIA GPU</b> with a current driver. The shipped profile is tuned for 8 GB of video memory.</li>
        <li>
          <b>Model weights and llama.cpp.</b> Not included. <a class="link" href={doc('guides/serve-models')}>The models guide</a> lists the files, their sources and their SHA-256 values.
        </li>
        <li><b>Git for Windows</b>, recommended. Git Bash is the terminal’s default shell.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="start__note">The program is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may warn the first time. Check the download’s hash first.</p>

      <p class="hero__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href={links.releases} rel="noopener"><Icon name="download" size={16} /> Download for Windows</a>
        <a class="btn" href={doc('guides/install')}>Install guide</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="start__steps" data-start>
      <div class="paths" role="tablist" aria-label="How to install">
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="way-zip-tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="way-zip" data-way="zip">Release zip</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="way-src-tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="way-src" tabindex="-1" data-way="src">From source</button>
        <button type="button" role="tab" id="way-oc-tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="way-oc" tabindex="-1" data-way="oc">With OpenCode</button>
      </div>

      <div class="way" id="way-zip" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="way-zip-tab">
        <p><code>{zipName}</code> holds <code>bridle.exe</code>, an installer, a short README, the licences and a checksum file.</p>
        <Command
          label="cmd.exe"
          lines={[
            '::  in the unpacked folder: check the program, then install it',
            'certutil -hashfile bridle.exe SHA256',
            'install.cmd',
            '::  in a new terminal, open a project',
            'cd path\\to\\your\\project',
            'bridle',
          ]}
        />
        <ol>
          <li>Compare the hash with <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> before you run it.</li>
          <li><code>install.cmd</code> copies <code>bridle.exe</code> to <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Bridle\bin</code> and adds that folder to your user <code>PATH</code>. Delete the folder to undo it.</li>
          <li>The first time on a computer, Bridle asks where your models and runtimes are. The first time in a project, it asks before it writes <code>bridle.toml</code>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="way" id="way-src" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="way-src-tab" hidden>
        <p>Needs Visual Studio 2022 with the C++ workload and Rust. <code>rustup</code> installs the version pinned in <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>.</p>
        <Command
          label="cmd.exe"
          lines={['git clone https://github.com/kushagrathisside/bridle.git', 'cd bridle', 'scripts\\dev.cmd release', 'scripts\\dev.cmd install']}
        />
        <ol>
          <li><code>scripts\dev.cmd gate</code> runs the format check, clippy with warnings as errors, and the tests, as CI does.</li>
          <li><a class="link" href={doc('project/contributing')}>Contributing</a> says what a change must pass.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="way" id="way-oc" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="way-oc-tab" hidden>
        <p>Prefer OpenCode’s interface? Bridle’s policy sits around it. This is written for OpenCode 1.19.3.</p>
        <Command
          label="cmd.exe · in a project"
          lines={['bridle init --profile local-only', 'bridle config validate', 'opencode run "Describe the files in this repository."']}
        />
        <ol>
          <li><code>bridle init</code> writes <code>bridle.toml</code>, <code>opencode.json</code> and the plugin, and ignores <code>.bridle/</code> in Git.</li>
          <li>OpenCode loads the plugin, so every tool call goes through the same policy.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <p class="spec__source">
        Check the install with <code>bridle --version</code>, which prints <code>bridle {version}</code>, and <code>bridle doctor</code>, which checks the machine.
        <a class="link" href={doc('getting-started')}>The full walk-through</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/start';
</script>
